<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/6th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Taginae</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/552">552</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a> · Also known as Battle of Busta Gallorum · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gothic-war-535-554">Gothic War (535–554)</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Taginae, also called Busta Gallorum, was fought in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apennines">Apennines</a> of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in the summer of 552. A Byzantine army under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eunuch">eunuch</a> general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narses">Narses</a> defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ostrogoths">Ostrogoths</a>, and their king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/totila">Totila</a> was mortally wounded. The battle broke Gothic power in Italy and opened the way for the final Byzantine reconquest of the peninsula.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-taginae.jpg" alt="Battle of Taginae" width="320" height="351" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: A.Cython · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gothic_War_army_movements_second_phase.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>After the murder of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amalasuntha">Amalasuntha</a> in 535 Justinian used her death as a pretext to invade <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belisarius">Belisarius</a> took Ravenna in May 540. Byzantine misrule strengthened the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a>, and under their king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/totila">Totila</a> they held much of southern and central Italy by 544. Totila captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in December 546. Justinian assigned his cousin Germanus to raise an army, but Germanus died in 550, and in mid-551 Justinian appointed his chamberlain <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narses">Narses</a>. The force assembled at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amfissa">Salona</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalmatia">Dalmatia</a> included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombards</a>, Heruls and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgars">Bulgars</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> controlled the land route through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venetia">Venetia</a>, so Narses marched along the Adriatic coast while a fleet ferried troops across river mouths, and he reached Ravenna.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>In July 552 the armies met at Busta Gallorum, near the village of Taginae, north of modern Gualdo Tadino. Totila waited there for 2,000 men under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teia">Teia</a>. Narses deployed defensively, with dismounted Germanic mercenaries in a dense centre, 4,000 foot-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">archers</a> on each wing and a detachment of 1,500 cavalry behind a hill on the left. The next day the Armenian bodyguard Anzalas killed the Goth Coccas in single combat, and Totila rode out in shining purple and gold armour, tossing his lance, to delay the battle. When Teia's men arrived, Totila withdrew his troops for lunch, hoping to relax the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a>, but Narses kept his army in position.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/totila">Totila</a> then formed his cavalry in a single body, with the infantry behind, and charged the Byzantine centre with orders to use spears over any other weapon. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narses">Narses</a> turned his two archer detachments half round into a crescent, so that they shot at the Gothic cavalry from both sides. The charge faltered with heavy losses, and by evening the cavalry gave way. The Gothic infantry, which had not fought, fled with it. As many as 6,000 were slain by nightfall, many prisoners among them put to death, and Totila was mortally wounded.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narses">Narses</a> dismissed his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> allies after their arson and rape, and entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in July 552. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a> proclaimed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teia">Teia</a> king, and he was defeated and killed at Mons Lactarius near Vesuvius in October. In October 554 Narses defeated a Frankish army at the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volturno">Volturno</a>. Taginae was not the last engagement, but it broke the ability of the Goths to field a viable army.</p><p>Charles <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oman">Oman</a> called it the first experiment in the combination of pike and bow. Philip <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rance">Rance</a> rejects that reading, arguing that infantry remained the core of Byzantine armies, and that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/totila">Totila</a> had lost strategically before the battle began, because he had to intercept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narses">Narses</a>'s numerically superior army before it reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. The main source is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/procopius">Procopius</a>, whose account emphasises mounted combat.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Taginae” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Totila was killed at <b>Busta Gallorum</b> in 552, and Teia at Mons Lactarius in 552 or 553.</blockquote><a href="/state/ostrogothic-kingdom">Ostrogothic Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
